  "tldr": "Constant-CATE testing is posed as a sharp minimax problem over the bounded observational Hölder class, with the unknown constant profiled rather than fixed at zero and without an interior arm-mean restriction. A coercive conditional-moment identity and known-design augmented projections lead to an explicit higher-order cross-energy test with the candidate upper rate \\(R_{\\mathrm{up}}=n^{-2\\gamma/(4\\gamma+d)}\\vee n^{-4\\gamma(\\alpha+\\beta)/(4\\gamma(\\alpha+\\beta)+d(\\alpha+\\beta)+2d\\gamma)}\\). The central open theorem is to derive the actual same-class rate, including all phase boundaries and boundary logarithms, by matching or improving this test with legal full-observation mixtures whose null components retain constant CATE and exact uniform design. A companion phase map will locate Yu's learner-remainder guarantee and Dhawan--Guo--Shah's direction-correlation guarantee relative to the sharp uniform radius, making their non-subsumption operational.",
  "project_justification": {
    "gap": "Existing observational homogeneity tests establish asymptotic size and power under learned-direction, learner-remainder, or multisample U-statistic conditions; existing Hölder minimax theory treats CATE estimation, direct regression, conditional-mean independence under different classes, or heteroskedasticity rather than the declared constant-CATE experiment.",
    "niche": "The missing object is the full-class minimax separation radius when the null contains every unknown constant effect and arbitrary permitted rough propensity and baseline functions, including regimes in which both useful sieve ranks exceed sample size and first-order product conditions fail.",
    "fill": "The project combines a profiled bounded-weight score, deterministic local-polynomial product correction, independent higher-order cross-energy, and a null-preserving full-likelihood mixture program to produce a computable upper bound and the matching observational converse on one unchanged class."
  },
  "related_work": "Dhawan, Guo and Shah (2026), Corollary 2 and equation (17), give power when the hunted direction has correlation exceeding \\(\\rho_n\\) with the population score and \\(n\\rho_n^2\\epsilon_n\\to\\infty\\); this is favorable-direction power, not a uniform omnibus radius. Yu (2026), Assumption 4 and Theorem 1, directly targets CATE variance with intra-fold splitting under separate \\(o_P(n^{-1/4})\\) propensity, outcome-regression, and CATE learner errors and a root-\\(n\\) ATE error; it does not derive those learners from the Hölder class or give a minimax converse, and Assumption 3(ii) imposes boundary nondegeneracy absent from the full bounded-outcome class here. The phase-comparison question requires legal witnesses showing where neither sufficient regime contains all alternatives detectable at the sharp uniform radius, while allowing that directional alignment can yield different pointwise power. Crump, Hotz, Imbens and Mitnik (2008) provide the classical constant-CATE test. Dai, Shen and Stern (2023), Canadian Journal of Statistics 51(2):531--558, provide a propensity-reweighted multisample U-statistic test for observational treatment-effect heterogeneity that bypasses modeling the treatment effect in each study subgroup; they do not characterize the continuous Hölder minimax frontier. Kennedy, Balakrishnan, Robins and Wasserman (2024) supply the closest causal minimax geometry and localized fuzzy-hypothesis method, but their target is pointwise CATE estimation and their hard design construction cannot be imported under exact uniform design. Kotekal and Kundu (2025), Annals of Statistics 53(1):295--321, show that a proxy statistic can be minimax for heteroskedasticity testing with a rough mean, motivating the search for a causal proxy while also showing why unrestricted bounded outcome noise must be handled explicitly. Comminges and Dalalyan (2013) and Ingster and Sapatinas (2009) provide the quadratic U-test benchmark after causal nuisance is removed. Luedtke, Carone and van der Laan (2019) provide a second-order U-statistic construction and degenerate null law for equality of unknown functions. Lundborg, Kim, Shah and Samworth (2024) prove a projected-covariance minimax result under a different common-smoothness and nondegeneracy regime. Dukes, Stensrud, Brioschi and Hudson (2024) and Sánchez-Becerra (2023) clarify policy-sensitive and boundary-robust homogeneity inference, but not this rough-confounding separation problem.",
  "technical_internal_limitation": "The displayed \\(R_{\\mathrm{up}}\\) is an upper-envelope candidate. The presolve's coefficient-covariance bound, simultaneous profiling constants, and numerical basis constants still require complete proof. More importantly, the oracle lower bound proves no nuisance penalty and cannot certify \\(R_{\\mathrm{up}}\\): at \\(d=1\\), \\(\\alpha=1/64\\), \\(\\beta=9/64\\), and \\(\\gamma=1/8\\), a valid converse must preserve baseline smoothness while accounting for every transition region observed under uniform design. Failure of one mixture construction is not evidence that the nuisance exponent is false.",
  "honest_scope": "The target class keeps exact uniform design, outcomes supported on \\([0,1]\\), fixed strong overlap, known class constants, and separate Hölder smoothness for \\(e\\), \\(\\mu_0\\), and \\(\\tau\\). It imposes no uniform interior bound on \\(\\mu_0\\) or \\(\\mu_1\\) and no conditional-variance lower bound; the affine Bernoulli witness is deliberately interior only to provide perturbation slack for lower bounds, and any lower-bound prior may be confined to that submodel while the upper bound remains uniform over the larger bounded-outcome class. Unknown design density, smoothness adaptation, and outcomes outside \\([0,1]\\) are outside scope. The known-propensity exponent and the explicit observational upper-bound formula are theorem targets; the matching observational rate, individual smoothness dependence, boundary logarithms, and the Yu/Dhawan--Guo--Shah non-subsumption map are open. Under supplied propensity, the proposed frontier must reduce to \\(n^{-2\\gamma/(4\\gamma+d)}\\).",
